# The Nodes list's sort chip: what order the rows the filter kept are in, on the same bar as the
# chips that decide which rows those are.
#
# Four things to watch, and the fourth is the one that would be a bug nobody could see.
#
#   - The sort is the last chip on the bar and says the order it is set to, so the list's state
#     reads at a glance. Five orders are too many to lay out as chips beside the filters, so the
#     chip is the word and A on it raises a sheet with all five, the current one checked.
#   - The sheet is a choice rather than a step. Up and Down move, A picks and closes, B closes
#     with nothing changed - so going one too far costs a press back up the sheet, not a lap of
#     all five orders.
#   - The heading does not change as the order changes. A filter changes how many rows there
#     are and a sort does not, which is the difference the two kinds of chip exist to keep apart.
#   - Under Distance the rows reorder, and the cursor still opens the node the row drew rather
#     than the one that used to be there.

scene demo
scale 4
clock 2026-01-12 19:12
config

pin ECHO

tab nodes
hold 1600

# Along the bar to the sort chip. The walk changes nothing - the filter stays on All, so
# everything that moves below is the order.
key right 3
hold 1000

# A raises the sheet: the five orders, Default checked.
key a
hold 1600

# Default -> Recent: last heard first, the plain question the phone apps open on. On this roster
# the client's own ranking already agrees with it down the first screenful, and the chip is what
# says which of the two questions the list is answering.
key down
hold 700
key a
hold 2200

# Recent -> Name. The answer to the search a reader would rather not type: a name you would know
# on sight, at a place you can scroll to.
key a
hold 900
key down
hold 700
key a
hold 2200

# Name -> Distance. The one a filter genuinely cannot answer - "near me" is an ordering of all of
# them rather than a kind of node.
key a
hold 900
key down
hold 700
key a
hold 2400

# And a change of mind. The sheet opens on Distance, the cursor moves, and B closes it with the
# order left where it was.
key a
hold 900
key up
hold 700
key b
hold 1600

# The rows under it are still rows. The cursor walks off the bar into them and A opens the node
# the row drew, which is the half that would look right while being wrong. The fifth row under
# Distance is Bravo Creek; the fifth under Default was Delta Camp.
key down 5
hold 1200
key a
hold 1800
key b
hold 700
